1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,787
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,967 --> 00:00:04,867
In Ernest Hemingway's novel The Sun Also

3
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:07,767
Rises, one character asks another how he

4
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:08,782
went bankrupt.

5
00:00:08,962 --> 00:00:10,704
The answer: two ways.

6
00:00:10,884 --> 00:00:12,625
Gradually, then suddenly.

7
00:00:12,805 --> 00:00:14,083
Here's the good news.

8
00:00:14,263 --> 00:00:16,399
Wealth works exactly the same way.

9
00:00:16,579 --> 00:00:18,460
Slowly, then suddenly.

10
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,129
Today, the three money habits that build

11
00:00:21,129 --> 00:00:22,125
wealth that way,

12
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:24,427
and why most people quit right before

13
00:00:24,427 --> 00:00:25,548
the suddenly part.

14
00:00:25,898 --> 00:00:26,977
Three habits.

15
00:00:27,157 --> 00:00:29,362
Spend less than you earn and automate

16
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:29,839
the gap.

17
00:00:30,019 --> 00:00:31,992
Invest that gap consistently,

18
00:00:31,992 --> 00:00:33,421
for a very long time.

19
00:00:33,601 --> 00:00:35,052
And keep growing what you earn.

20
00:00:35,232 --> 00:00:35,708
That's it.

21
00:00:36,059 --> 00:00:36,558
I know.

22
00:00:36,738 --> 00:00:38,073
That sounds too simple.

23
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:39,356
Where's the secret?

24
00:00:39,536 --> 00:00:40,438
The hot stock,

25
00:00:40,438 --> 00:00:41,470
the side hustle,

26
00:00:41,470 --> 00:00:42,566
the clever trick?

27
00:00:42,746 --> 00:00:43,791
Here's the truth.

28
00:00:43,971 --> 00:00:44,760
There isn't one.

29
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:47,019
The people who build real wealth on

30
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:49,752
ordinary incomes almost always do some version

31
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:51,059
of these three things.

32
00:00:51,239 --> 00:00:54,001
What separates them from everyone else isn't

33
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:54,629
knowledge.

34
00:00:54,809 --> 00:00:56,931
It's that they keep doing them for

35
00:00:56,931 --> 00:00:59,052
years, especially during the long,

36
00:00:59,052 --> 00:01:01,798
boring stretch when nothing seems to happen.

37
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:04,460
We'll look at these three habits from

38
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:05,272
three angles.

39
00:01:05,452 --> 00:01:06,801
First, the numbers:

40
00:01:06,801 --> 00:01:08,646
the math that turns small,

41
00:01:08,646 --> 00:01:11,131
boring habits into a sudden jump in

42
00:01:11,131 --> 00:01:11,628
wealth.

43
00:01:11,808 --> 00:01:13,329
Second, the psychology:

44
00:01:13,329 --> 00:01:15,378
why these habits are so hard to

45
00:01:15,378 --> 00:01:17,956
keep, and what habit science says about

46
00:01:17,956 --> 00:01:19,146
making them stick.

47
00:01:19,326 --> 00:01:21,095
And third, the playbook:

48
00:01:21,095 --> 00:01:23,601
a thirty day setup for each habit,

49
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:25,444
so they run on autopilot.

50
00:01:25,794 --> 00:01:27,775
Stay for the chart in the numbers

51
00:01:27,775 --> 00:01:28,255
section.

52
00:01:28,435 --> 00:01:31,125
It shows exactly where the suddenly happens,

53
00:01:31,125 --> 00:01:33,510
and it's usually much later than people

54
00:01:33,510 --> 00:01:33,938
expect.

55
00:01:34,118 --> 00:01:35,024
Let's start.

56
00:01:35,374 --> 00:01:37,668
Every number here is either from a

57
00:01:37,668 --> 00:01:39,219
named source or stated,

58
00:01:39,219 --> 00:01:40,029
simple math.

59
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:43,143
Growth examples use a seven percent average

60
00:01:43,143 --> 00:01:45,872
yearly return, which is an illustration,

61
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:46,827
not a promise.

62
00:01:47,007 --> 00:01:48,478
Markets go up and down,

63
00:01:48,478 --> 00:01:50,269
and some years are negative.

64
00:01:50,449 --> 00:01:53,593
Research on habits comes from published studies,

65
00:01:53,593 --> 00:01:56,475
including work at University College London.

66
00:01:56,655 --> 00:01:59,360
Retirement rules come from the IRS.

67
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:00,938
This is education,

68
00:02:00,938 --> 00:02:02,570
not financial advice.

69
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,564
Hemingway's line was about going broke,

70
00:02:05,564 --> 00:02:08,342
but it describes almost every big change.

71
00:02:08,522 --> 00:02:11,076
A forest grows slowly for decades,

72
00:02:11,076 --> 00:02:13,480
then seems to fill the hillside.

73
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:15,952
A skill improves slowly for years,

74
00:02:15,952 --> 00:02:18,919
then suddenly you're the expert people call.

75
00:02:19,099 --> 00:02:21,658
In money, the slow part can last

76
00:02:21,658 --> 00:02:23,337
ten or fifteen years.

77
00:02:23,517 --> 00:02:26,079
The sudden part is when compounding finally

78
00:02:26,079 --> 00:02:28,522
becomes visible, and the growth starts to

79
00:02:28,522 --> 00:02:29,833
dwarf what you put in.

80
00:02:30,182 --> 00:02:32,574
Warren Buffett is the most famous example.

81
00:02:32,754 --> 00:02:34,940
Most of his enormous wealth was built

82
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:36,594
after his fiftieth birthday,

83
00:02:36,594 --> 00:02:38,662
not because he got smarter later in

84
00:02:38,662 --> 00:02:40,966
life, but because he started very young

85
00:02:40,966 --> 00:02:42,739
and never stopped compounding.

86
00:02:42,919 --> 00:02:45,073
The habits were in place for decades

87
00:02:45,073 --> 00:02:47,168
before the results became dramatic.

88
00:02:47,518 --> 00:02:48,929
You don't need to be Buffett for

89
00:02:48,929 --> 00:02:49,503
this to work.

90
00:02:49,683 --> 00:02:51,942
The same math applies to a teacher

91
00:02:51,942 --> 00:02:54,334
saving four hundred dollars a month,

92
00:02:54,334 --> 00:02:56,659
or a nurse investing ten percent of

93
00:02:56,659 --> 00:02:57,589
each paycheck.

94
00:02:57,769 --> 00:02:59,023
The scale is different.

95
00:02:59,203 --> 00:03:00,847
The shape of the curve is the

96
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:01,130
same.

97
00:03:01,310 --> 00:03:02,982
A long, flat start,

98
00:03:02,982 --> 00:03:04,654
then a steep climb.

99
00:03:05,003 --> 00:03:05,770
Angle one.

100
00:03:05,950 --> 00:03:06,751
The numbers.

101
00:03:06,931 --> 00:03:08,993
Let's look at the math behind each

102
00:03:08,993 --> 00:03:11,359
habit, starting with the one that makes

103
00:03:11,359 --> 00:03:12,875
everything else possible.

104
00:03:13,225 --> 00:03:13,864
Habit one.

105
00:03:14,044 --> 00:03:15,495
Spend less than you earn,

106
00:03:15,495 --> 00:03:17,121
and automate the difference.

107
00:03:17,301 --> 00:03:19,999
The gap between income and spending is

108
00:03:19,999 --> 00:03:22,200
the raw material of all wealth.

109
00:03:22,550 --> 00:03:25,086
The most important number in personal finance

110
00:03:25,086 --> 00:03:27,566
isn't your income or your investment return.

111
00:03:27,746 --> 00:03:28,926
It's your savings rate,

112
00:03:28,926 --> 00:03:30,927
the percentage of your income you keep.

113
00:03:31,107 --> 00:03:33,950
Someone earning sixty thousand dollars who saves

114
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:36,674
fifteen percent is building wealth faster than

115
00:03:36,674 --> 00:03:39,399
someone earning one hundred fifty thousand who

116
00:03:39,399 --> 00:03:40,465
saves two percent.

117
00:03:40,814 --> 00:03:43,984
Here's why savings rate is so powerful.

118
00:03:44,164 --> 00:03:45,971
It works on both sides of the

119
00:03:45,971 --> 00:03:46,532
equation.

120
00:03:46,712 --> 00:03:47,872
When you save more,

121
00:03:47,872 --> 00:03:49,580
you have more money growing.

122
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,610
And you're also living on less,

123
00:03:51,610 --> 00:03:53,937
which means you need less to eventually

124
00:03:53,937 --> 00:03:55,310
support your lifestyle.

125
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:58,100
A higher savings rate shortens the journey

126
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:59,530
from both ends at once.

127
00:03:59,879 --> 00:04:01,191
Let's put numbers on that.

128
00:04:01,371 --> 00:04:03,120
Using a simple illustration,

129
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,742
with investments growing at five percent a

130
00:04:05,742 --> 00:04:07,053
year after inflation,

131
00:04:07,053 --> 00:04:09,051
and a goal of having twenty five

132
00:04:09,051 --> 00:04:10,737
times your yearly spending,

133
00:04:10,737 --> 00:04:13,110
here's roughly how many years it takes

134
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:16,357
to reach financial independence at different savings

135
00:04:16,357 --> 00:04:16,731
rates.

136
00:04:16,911 --> 00:04:18,644
At five percent saved,

137
00:04:18,644 --> 00:04:20,534
around sixty five years.

138
00:04:20,714 --> 00:04:22,022
At fifteen percent,

139
00:04:22,022 --> 00:04:23,744
around forty three years.

140
00:04:24,093 --> 00:04:25,366
At twenty percent,

141
00:04:25,366 --> 00:04:27,205
around thirty seven years.

142
00:04:27,385 --> 00:04:28,561
At thirty percent,

143
00:04:28,561 --> 00:04:30,195
about twenty eight years.

144
00:04:30,375 --> 00:04:31,539
At fifty percent,

145
00:04:31,539 --> 00:04:33,115
around seventeen years.

146
00:04:33,295 --> 00:04:35,774
Notice how the time drops sharply as

147
00:04:35,774 --> 00:04:37,358
the savings rate rises.

148
00:04:37,538 --> 00:04:39,825
Going from five to fifteen percent cuts

149
00:04:39,825 --> 00:04:42,054
more than two decades off the journey.

150
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:43,895
That's the power of habit one.

151
00:04:44,244 --> 00:04:46,434
Years to financial independence,

152
00:04:46,434 --> 00:04:47,734
as an illustration,

153
00:04:47,734 --> 00:04:51,223
assuming five percent yearly growth after inflation

154
00:04:51,223 --> 00:04:53,343
and a goal of twenty five times

155
00:04:53,343 --> 00:04:54,438
yearly spending.

156
00:04:54,787 --> 00:04:56,656
The second half of habit one is

157
00:04:56,656 --> 00:04:57,318
automation.

158
00:04:57,498 --> 00:05:00,745
Researchers studying retirement savings have found that

159
00:05:00,745 --> 00:05:04,877
automatic enrollment dramatically increases participation in workplace

160
00:05:04,877 --> 00:05:05,231
plans.

161
00:05:05,411 --> 00:05:06,943
When saving is the default,

162
00:05:06,943 --> 00:05:08,418
most people stick with it.

163
00:05:08,598 --> 00:05:10,371
When it requires action,

164
00:05:10,371 --> 00:05:11,628
many never start.

165
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:14,075
Automation turns a decision you'd have to

166
00:05:14,075 --> 00:05:15,955
make every month into one you make

167
00:05:15,955 --> 00:05:16,231
once.

168
00:05:16,581 --> 00:05:17,557
Habit two.

169
00:05:17,737 --> 00:05:19,684
Invest the gap consistently,

170
00:05:19,684 --> 00:05:22,257
in diversified, low cost investments,

171
00:05:22,257 --> 00:05:24,761
and don't interrupt the compounding.

172
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:26,468
This is where slowly,

173
00:05:26,468 --> 00:05:28,408
then suddenly becomes visible.

174
00:05:28,588 --> 00:05:31,682
Imagine investing five hundred dollars a month

175
00:05:31,682 --> 00:05:34,439
at a seven percent average yearly return.

176
00:05:34,619 --> 00:05:35,558
After ten years,

177
00:05:35,558 --> 00:05:37,965
you'd have put in sixty thousand dollars,

178
00:05:37,965 --> 00:05:40,195
and the balance might be around eighty

179
00:05:40,195 --> 00:05:40,958
six thousand.

180
00:05:41,138 --> 00:05:43,193
Nice, but not life changing.

181
00:05:43,373 --> 00:05:44,659
This is the slow part,

182
00:05:44,659 --> 00:05:46,705
and it's where many people give up.

183
00:05:47,055 --> 00:05:48,163
After twenty years,

184
00:05:48,163 --> 00:05:50,263
you'd have put in one hundred twenty

185
00:05:50,263 --> 00:05:52,712
thousand dollars, and the balance could be

186
00:05:52,712 --> 00:05:54,695
around two hundred sixty thousand.

187
00:05:54,875 --> 00:05:56,885
Now growth is doing more than half

188
00:05:56,885 --> 00:05:57,417
the work.

189
00:05:57,597 --> 00:05:58,864
After thirty years,

190
00:05:58,864 --> 00:06:00,929
about six hundred ten thousand.

191
00:06:01,109 --> 00:06:02,509
And after forty years,

192
00:06:02,509 --> 00:06:05,055
roughly one point three million dollars,

193
00:06:05,055 --> 00:06:07,855
from two hundred forty thousand contributed.

194
00:06:08,204 --> 00:06:10,001
Five hundred dollars a month,

195
00:06:10,001 --> 00:06:12,542
at a seven percent average yearly return,

196
00:06:12,542 --> 00:06:13,533
for forty years.

197
00:06:13,713 --> 00:06:15,997
You'd put in two hundred forty thousand

198
00:06:15,997 --> 00:06:16,465
dollars.

199
00:06:16,645 --> 00:06:18,166
Look at the shape of the curve.

200
00:06:18,515 --> 00:06:20,014
Look at the last ten years on

201
00:06:20,014 --> 00:06:20,582
that chart.

202
00:06:20,762 --> 00:06:22,556
The balance more than doubles,

203
00:06:22,556 --> 00:06:24,828
from about six hundred thousand to one

204
00:06:24,828 --> 00:06:26,023
point three million,

205
00:06:26,023 --> 00:06:28,475
while you only add sixty thousand dollars

206
00:06:28,475 --> 00:06:29,551
of your own money.

207
00:06:29,731 --> 00:06:30,729
That's the suddenly.

208
00:06:30,909 --> 00:06:32,477
And here's the critical point.

209
00:06:32,657 --> 00:06:34,353
You only get those last ten years

210
00:06:34,353 --> 00:06:36,151
if you didn't quit during the first

211
00:06:36,151 --> 00:06:36,511
twenty.

212
00:06:36,861 --> 00:06:38,933
That's why the second half of habit

213
00:06:38,933 --> 00:06:40,117
two matters so much:

214
00:06:40,117 --> 00:06:41,064
don't interrupt.

215
00:06:41,244 --> 00:06:43,899
The biggest threats to compounding are not

216
00:06:43,899 --> 00:06:44,658
bad markets.

217
00:06:44,838 --> 00:06:47,314
They're people pulling money out early,

218
00:06:47,314 --> 00:06:49,918
stopping contributions during a downturn,

219
00:06:49,918 --> 00:06:52,459
or cashing out a retirement account when

220
00:06:52,459 --> 00:06:53,348
changing jobs.

221
00:06:53,528 --> 00:06:56,141
Each interruption resets part of the curve

222
00:06:56,141 --> 00:06:57,510
back to the slow part.

223
00:06:57,859 --> 00:06:58,753
And keep it cheap.

224
00:06:58,933 --> 00:07:01,189
A one percent difference in yearly fees

225
00:07:01,189 --> 00:07:02,231
might sound small.

226
00:07:02,411 --> 00:07:03,719
But over forty years,

227
00:07:03,719 --> 00:07:05,837
it can reduce the final balance by

228
00:07:05,837 --> 00:07:06,772
a large amount,

229
00:07:06,772 --> 00:07:08,703
often well over twenty percent.

230
00:07:08,883 --> 00:07:11,715
Low cost index funds became popular for

231
00:07:11,715 --> 00:07:13,167
exactly this reason.

232
00:07:13,347 --> 00:07:15,008
You can't control the market.

233
00:07:15,188 --> 00:07:16,558
You can control what you pay.

234
00:07:16,907 --> 00:07:19,129
There's a specific moment in the curve

235
00:07:19,129 --> 00:07:20,298
worth knowing about.

236
00:07:20,478 --> 00:07:22,207
I call it the crossover point.

237
00:07:22,387 --> 00:07:24,523
It's the year when your investments earn

238
00:07:24,523 --> 00:07:26,177
more in a typical year than you

239
00:07:26,177 --> 00:07:26,764
contribute.

240
00:07:26,944 --> 00:07:27,883
In our example,

241
00:07:27,883 --> 00:07:30,200
five hundred dollars a month at seven

242
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:33,016
percent, that moment arrives around year ten.

243
00:07:33,196 --> 00:07:33,951
From then on,

244
00:07:33,951 --> 00:07:35,054
in an average year,

245
00:07:35,054 --> 00:07:37,028
your money is adding more than you

246
00:07:37,028 --> 00:07:37,260
are.

247
00:07:37,609 --> 00:07:38,841
Before the crossover,

248
00:07:38,841 --> 00:07:39,897
you're the engine.

249
00:07:40,077 --> 00:07:42,410
Every dollar of progress comes mostly from

250
00:07:42,410 --> 00:07:43,188
your paycheck.

251
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:44,591
After the crossover,

252
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:45,874
you have two engines,

253
00:07:45,874 --> 00:07:48,442
and the second one keeps getting stronger.

254
00:07:48,622 --> 00:07:49,617
By year twenty,

255
00:07:49,617 --> 00:07:51,742
growth in an average year can be

256
00:07:51,742 --> 00:07:54,264
two or three times your contributions.

257
00:07:54,444 --> 00:07:55,546
By year thirty,

258
00:07:55,546 --> 00:07:57,602
it can be many times larger.

259
00:07:57,951 --> 00:08:00,912
Of course, real markets don't deliver average

260
00:08:00,912 --> 00:08:01,307
years.

261
00:08:01,487 --> 00:08:03,264
Some years the growth is huge,

262
00:08:03,264 --> 00:08:04,981
and some years it's negative.

263
00:08:05,161 --> 00:08:06,688
But over long periods,

264
00:08:06,688 --> 00:08:08,354
the shape tends to hold.

265
00:08:08,534 --> 00:08:10,856
And knowing that the crossover is coming

266
00:08:10,856 --> 00:08:13,062
makes the slow years easier to accept.

267
00:08:13,242 --> 00:08:14,206
You're not failing.

268
00:08:14,386 --> 00:08:16,023
You're building the first engine.

269
00:08:16,372 --> 00:08:18,132
Now let's see what happens if the

270
00:08:18,132 --> 00:08:19,252
habit is interrupted.

271
00:08:19,432 --> 00:08:22,206
Same example, five hundred dollars a month

272
00:08:22,206 --> 00:08:23,263
for forty years.

273
00:08:23,443 --> 00:08:24,393
But this time,

274
00:08:24,393 --> 00:08:27,243
the person stops investing for five years,

275
00:08:27,243 --> 00:08:29,008
from year six to year ten,

276
00:08:29,008 --> 00:08:30,500
because life got busy,

277
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,197
or the market felt scary.

278
00:08:32,377 --> 00:08:35,314
They skip thirty thousand dollars of contributions.

279
00:08:35,664 --> 00:08:37,534
The result after forty years?

280
00:08:37,714 --> 00:08:40,340
Instead of about one point three million

281
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:43,228
dollars, roughly one point zero two million.

282
00:08:43,408 --> 00:08:46,618
Skipping thirty thousand dollars of contributions early

283
00:08:46,618 --> 00:08:49,245
on cost nearly three hundred thousand dollars

284
00:08:49,245 --> 00:08:49,887
at the end.

285
00:08:50,067 --> 00:08:52,533
Early dollars are the most valuable dollars,

286
00:08:52,533 --> 00:08:54,439
because they have the most time to

287
00:08:54,439 --> 00:08:54,943
compound.

288
00:08:55,292 --> 00:08:57,455
Five hundred dollars a month for forty

289
00:08:57,455 --> 00:08:58,764
years at seven percent.

290
00:08:58,944 --> 00:09:00,978
A five year pause early on skips

291
00:09:00,978 --> 00:09:03,584
thirty thousand dollars of contributions,

292
00:09:03,584 --> 00:09:05,872
but can reduce the ending balance by

293
00:09:05,872 --> 00:09:07,779
nearly three hundred thousand.

294
00:09:08,128 --> 00:09:10,057
This is why habit two says don't

295
00:09:10,057 --> 00:09:10,659
interrupt.

296
00:09:10,839 --> 00:09:12,134
If money gets tight,

297
00:09:12,134 --> 00:09:15,692
reduce the contribution instead of stopping completely.

298
00:09:15,872 --> 00:09:18,084
Even fifty dollars a month keeps the

299
00:09:18,084 --> 00:09:20,296
habit alive and the account growing.

300
00:09:20,476 --> 00:09:23,451
A smaller contribution is almost always better

301
00:09:23,451 --> 00:09:24,098
than none.

302
00:09:24,448 --> 00:09:26,551
Let's look at what research and data

303
00:09:26,551 --> 00:09:28,419
say about everyday millionaires.

304
00:09:28,599 --> 00:09:31,866
Fidelity, one of the largest retirement plan

305
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:33,426
providers in America,

306
00:09:33,426 --> 00:09:36,248
regularly reports how many of its 401k

307
00:09:36,248 --> 00:09:39,441
savers have balances of one million dollars

308
00:09:39,441 --> 00:09:40,035
or more.

309
00:09:40,215 --> 00:09:41,184
In recent years,

310
00:09:41,184 --> 00:09:43,364
that number has been in the hundreds

311
00:09:43,364 --> 00:09:44,151
of thousands.

312
00:09:44,331 --> 00:09:46,304
Many of them aren't executives.

313
00:09:46,484 --> 00:09:49,422
They're people who contributed steadily for decades.

314
00:09:49,771 --> 00:09:52,232
Fidelity has noted that many of these

315
00:09:52,232 --> 00:09:55,292
savers contributed a meaningful share of their

316
00:09:55,292 --> 00:09:57,819
pay, including employer contributions,

317
00:09:57,819 --> 00:09:58,884
for a long time,

318
00:09:58,884 --> 00:10:00,813
often more than twenty years.

319
00:10:00,993 --> 00:10:03,062
That's habit one and habit two,

320
00:10:03,062 --> 00:10:03,930
in real life.

321
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:06,293
Steady saving, steady investing,

322
00:10:06,293 --> 00:10:07,999
and no big interruptions.

323
00:10:08,349 --> 00:10:11,299
Thomas Stanley's research on millionaires found a

324
00:10:11,299 --> 00:10:12,262
similar pattern.

325
00:10:12,442 --> 00:10:14,523
Many lived below their means,

326
00:10:14,523 --> 00:10:16,747
avoided debt, bought used cars,

327
00:10:16,747 --> 00:10:18,398
and invested regularly.

328
00:10:18,578 --> 00:10:20,411
Their income was often good,

329
00:10:20,411 --> 00:10:21,852
but not extraordinary.

330
00:10:22,032 --> 00:10:23,797
Their habits were what made the difference.

331
00:10:24,146 --> 00:10:26,760
And here's what's notably absent from these

332
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:27,246
stories.

333
00:10:27,426 --> 00:10:28,820
Lucky stock picks.

334
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,323
Perfect market timing.

335
00:10:30,503 --> 00:10:32,105
Secret strategies.

336
00:10:32,285 --> 00:10:34,950
The common thread is boring consistency,

337
00:10:34,950 --> 00:10:36,883
applied for a very long time.

338
00:10:37,063 --> 00:10:40,830
That's encouraging, because consistency is something almost

339
00:10:40,830 --> 00:10:41,916
anyone can learn.

340
00:10:42,265 --> 00:10:43,833
What about bad decades?

341
00:10:44,013 --> 00:10:44,617
They happen.

342
00:10:44,797 --> 00:10:47,468
From two thousand to two thousand nine,

343
00:10:47,468 --> 00:10:49,797
a period that included the dot com

344
00:10:49,797 --> 00:10:51,920
crash and the financial crisis,

345
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:53,975
the S and P five hundred ended

346
00:10:53,975 --> 00:10:56,236
the decade lower than it started,

347
00:10:56,236 --> 00:10:58,496
even with dividends roughly flat.

348
00:10:58,676 --> 00:11:00,615
People called it the lost decade.

349
00:11:00,965 --> 00:11:03,364
But someone who kept investing every month

350
00:11:03,364 --> 00:11:05,649
through that decade was buying shares at

351
00:11:05,649 --> 00:11:06,962
lower and lower prices,

352
00:11:06,962 --> 00:11:08,048
during the crashes.

353
00:11:08,228 --> 00:11:11,155
When markets recovered in the following decade,

354
00:11:11,155 --> 00:11:13,522
those cheap shares grew significantly.

355
00:11:13,702 --> 00:11:16,105
The habit didn't avoid the bad decade.

356
00:11:16,285 --> 00:11:18,502
It turned the bad decade into a

357
00:11:18,502 --> 00:11:19,861
buying opportunity.

358
00:11:20,210 --> 00:11:22,270
This is the deepest reason habit two

359
00:11:22,270 --> 00:11:22,614
works.

360
00:11:22,794 --> 00:11:24,367
You don't need to know what the

361
00:11:24,367 --> 00:11:25,534
next decade will bring.

362
00:11:25,714 --> 00:11:27,683
You just need to keep buying,

363
00:11:27,683 --> 00:11:28,701
keep costs low,

364
00:11:28,701 --> 00:11:31,553
stay diversified, and give it enough time.

365
00:11:31,733 --> 00:11:34,996
Past performance never guarantees future results.

366
00:11:35,176 --> 00:11:38,332
But consistent buying has historically been rewarded

367
00:11:38,332 --> 00:11:39,425
over long periods.

368
00:11:39,775 --> 00:11:40,855
Habit three.

369
00:11:41,035 --> 00:11:42,656
Keep growing your income,

370
00:11:42,656 --> 00:11:45,184
through skills, career moves and value,

371
00:11:45,184 --> 00:11:47,388
and send a share of every increase

372
00:11:47,388 --> 00:11:48,814
to habits one and two.

373
00:11:49,163 --> 00:11:51,353
Saving and investing have a limit set

374
00:11:51,353 --> 00:11:52,240
by your income.

375
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:54,127
You can only cut so much.

376
00:11:54,307 --> 00:11:56,605
But income can grow for decades.

377
00:11:56,785 --> 00:11:57,794
A new skill,

378
00:11:57,794 --> 00:12:00,316
a certification, a job change,

379
00:12:00,316 --> 00:12:02,753
a promotion, a side business.

380
00:12:02,933 --> 00:12:05,340
For most people early in their careers,

381
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:07,933
growing income is the single biggest lever

382
00:12:07,933 --> 00:12:09,168
for building wealth.

383
00:12:09,517 --> 00:12:11,423
Here's how it connects to the other

384
00:12:11,423 --> 00:12:11,805
habits.

385
00:12:11,985 --> 00:12:14,171
Say your income grows by three to

386
00:12:14,171 --> 00:12:16,622
five percent a year over your career,

387
00:12:16,622 --> 00:12:18,212
and every time it grows,

388
00:12:18,212 --> 00:12:20,133
you send half of the raise to

389
00:12:20,133 --> 00:12:20,994
your savings.

390
00:12:21,174 --> 00:12:23,535
Your savings rate climbs year after year

391
00:12:23,535 --> 00:12:26,073
without your lifestyle ever going backward.

392
00:12:26,253 --> 00:12:28,457
Habit three feeds habit one,

393
00:12:28,457 --> 00:12:30,189
which feeds habit two.

394
00:12:30,539 --> 00:12:32,697
And early raises matter more than late

395
00:12:32,697 --> 00:12:35,026
ones, for the same reason early investing

396
00:12:35,026 --> 00:12:35,764
matters more.

397
00:12:35,944 --> 00:12:38,548
A ten thousand dollar raise at twenty

398
00:12:38,548 --> 00:12:40,167
eight, partly invested,

399
00:12:40,167 --> 00:12:42,631
has nearly forty years to compound.

400
00:12:42,811 --> 00:12:44,939
The same raise at fifty eight has

401
00:12:44,939 --> 00:12:45,841
less than ten.

402
00:12:46,021 --> 00:12:48,339
Investing in your skills early is one

403
00:12:48,339 --> 00:12:51,095
of the highest return investments available.

404
00:12:51,445 --> 00:12:53,216
Here's a simple way to think about

405
00:12:53,216 --> 00:12:53,373
it.

406
00:12:53,553 --> 00:12:55,968
Every year, ask yourself one question.

407
00:12:56,148 --> 00:12:57,954
What could I learn or do this

408
00:12:57,954 --> 00:13:00,259
year that would make me more valuable

409
00:13:00,259 --> 00:13:02,314
to an employer or a customer next

410
00:13:02,314 --> 00:13:02,626
year?

411
00:13:02,806 --> 00:13:04,269
Then do one thing about it.

412
00:13:04,449 --> 00:13:05,338
Over a decade,

413
00:13:05,338 --> 00:13:07,369
those yearly answers add up to a

414
00:13:07,369 --> 00:13:08,766
very different career,

415
00:13:08,766 --> 00:13:10,544
and a very different income.

416
00:13:10,894 --> 00:13:12,694
And don't ignore negotiation.

417
00:13:12,874 --> 00:13:16,067
Many people accept the first salary offer.

418
00:13:16,247 --> 00:13:19,008
Research and surveys have suggested that people

419
00:13:19,008 --> 00:13:21,828
who negotiate often receive higher starting pay,

420
00:13:21,828 --> 00:13:23,826
and starting pay sets the base for

421
00:13:23,826 --> 00:13:24,943
every future raise.

422
00:13:25,123 --> 00:13:27,984
One uncomfortable ten minute conversation can be

423
00:13:27,984 --> 00:13:30,310
worth tens of thousands of dollars over

424
00:13:30,310 --> 00:13:30,846
a career.

425
00:13:31,196 --> 00:13:33,135
One more thought on the slow part.

426
00:13:33,315 --> 00:13:35,597
Many people quit because they compare their

427
00:13:35,597 --> 00:13:37,878
year three with someone else's year twenty.

428
00:13:38,058 --> 00:13:40,347
That's like comparing a seedling with an

429
00:13:40,347 --> 00:13:42,464
oak tree and deciding the seedling is

430
00:13:42,464 --> 00:13:42,864
broken.

431
00:13:43,044 --> 00:13:46,014
Every large balance you've ever seen online

432
00:13:46,014 --> 00:13:48,776
started as a small balance that somebody

433
00:13:48,776 --> 00:13:49,674
kept feeding.

434
00:13:49,854 --> 00:13:51,723
Give your seedling the same patience.

435
00:13:52,073 --> 00:13:53,780
And if you need a reminder,

436
00:13:53,780 --> 00:13:55,740
write down three numbers today:

437
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:56,878
your savings rate,

438
00:13:56,878 --> 00:13:59,597
your monthly investment and your net worth.

439
00:13:59,777 --> 00:14:01,565
Put the note away and look at

440
00:14:01,565 --> 00:14:02,737
it again in a year.

441
00:14:02,917 --> 00:14:05,362
Most people are surprised by how much

442
00:14:05,362 --> 00:14:07,740
the habits changed in twelve months,

443
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:10,382
even when the balance still feels small.

444
00:14:10,732 --> 00:14:11,975
So that's the math.

445
00:14:12,155 --> 00:14:14,329
Three habits, each simple,

446
00:14:14,329 --> 00:14:16,671
each powerful, and together,

447
00:14:16,671 --> 00:14:17,507
explosive.

448
00:14:17,687 --> 00:14:20,133
So why does almost nobody stick with

449
00:14:20,133 --> 00:14:20,473
them?

450
00:14:20,653 --> 00:14:21,570
That's angle two.

451
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:22,896
Angle two.

452
00:14:23,076 --> 00:14:24,237
The psychology.

453
00:14:24,417 --> 00:14:26,598
Eight reasons these habits are so hard,

454
00:14:26,598 --> 00:14:28,836
and what habit science says about fixing

455
00:14:28,836 --> 00:14:29,339
each one.

456
00:14:29,689 --> 00:14:30,421
Reason one.

457
00:14:30,601 --> 00:14:32,412
The slow part is boring.

458
00:14:32,592 --> 00:14:34,764
For years, your balance grows by amounts

459
00:14:34,764 --> 00:14:37,480
that feel meaningless compared with your paycheck.

460
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:40,017
Your brain craves visible progress.

461
00:14:40,197 --> 00:14:41,806
When it doesn't get any,

462
00:14:41,806 --> 00:14:44,288
it looks for something more exciting,

463
00:14:44,288 --> 00:14:45,428
like a hot stock,

464
00:14:45,428 --> 00:14:46,099
a new car,

465
00:14:46,099 --> 00:14:47,708
or a different strategy.

466
00:14:47,888 --> 00:14:50,338
Boredom is the silent killer of wealth.

467
00:14:50,687 --> 00:14:52,545
The fix: track the right number.

468
00:14:52,725 --> 00:14:54,692
Instead of watching the balance,

469
00:14:54,692 --> 00:14:57,150
track your savings rate and your monthly

470
00:14:57,150 --> 00:14:57,949
contribution.

471
00:14:58,129 --> 00:14:59,895
Those are things you control,

472
00:14:59,895 --> 00:15:01,357
and they improve faster.

473
00:15:01,537 --> 00:15:03,010
Celebrate the habit,

474
00:15:03,010 --> 00:15:04,114
not the result.

475
00:15:04,294 --> 00:15:05,839
The result will catch up.

476
00:15:06,188 --> 00:15:07,187
Reason two.

477
00:15:07,367 --> 00:15:08,423
Present bias.

478
00:15:08,603 --> 00:15:10,864
Your brain values a reward today much

479
00:15:10,864 --> 00:15:12,820
more than a bigger reward in the

480
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:13,247
future.

481
00:15:13,427 --> 00:15:15,321
Every month, the money you could save

482
00:15:15,321 --> 00:15:17,572
competes with something you could enjoy now.

483
00:15:17,752 --> 00:15:20,074
The present makes a strong argument,

484
00:15:20,074 --> 00:15:21,235
every single time.

485
00:15:21,585 --> 00:15:23,907
The fix: remove the monthly decision.

486
00:15:24,087 --> 00:15:26,481
Automate saving on payday so the present

487
00:15:26,481 --> 00:15:27,559
never gets a vote.

488
00:15:27,739 --> 00:15:31,692
Behavioral economists Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi

489
00:15:31,692 --> 00:15:34,868
designed a program called Save More Tomorrow,

490
00:15:34,868 --> 00:15:37,622
where workers commit in advance to save

491
00:15:37,622 --> 00:15:39,175
part of future raises.

492
00:15:39,355 --> 00:15:40,836
In the original study,

493
00:15:40,836 --> 00:15:44,405
participants' savings rates rose substantially over a

494
00:15:44,405 --> 00:15:45,078
few years.

495
00:15:45,428 --> 00:15:46,496
Reason three.

496
00:15:46,676 --> 00:15:47,652
Market fear.

497
00:15:47,832 --> 00:15:49,325
When markets drop,

498
00:15:49,325 --> 00:15:51,399
every instinct says sell,

499
00:15:51,399 --> 00:15:53,555
stop, protect what's left.

500
00:15:53,735 --> 00:15:55,746
But selling after a drop locks in

501
00:15:55,746 --> 00:15:58,792
the loss, and stopping contributions means missing

502
00:15:58,792 --> 00:16:00,864
the chance to buy at lower prices.

503
00:16:01,044 --> 00:16:03,796
Many of the worst investment decisions happen

504
00:16:03,796 --> 00:16:05,386
during the scariest weeks.

505
00:16:05,736 --> 00:16:08,063
The fix: write your plan before the

506
00:16:08,063 --> 00:16:08,395
drop.

507
00:16:08,575 --> 00:16:09,597
A simple note.

508
00:16:09,777 --> 00:16:12,010
When the market falls twenty percent,

509
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:13,882
I will keep investing as usual,

510
00:16:13,882 --> 00:16:14,908
and I won't sell.

511
00:16:15,088 --> 00:16:16,516
Read it when it happens.

512
00:16:16,696 --> 00:16:19,237
Decisions made calmly in advance are much

513
00:16:19,237 --> 00:16:21,468
better than decisions made in panic.

514
00:16:21,818 --> 00:16:22,817
Reason four.

515
00:16:22,997 --> 00:16:24,053
Comparison.

516
00:16:24,233 --> 00:16:25,923
Your friend made a fortune on a

517
00:16:25,923 --> 00:16:27,941
meme stock, or your neighbor bought a

518
00:16:27,941 --> 00:16:28,541
lake house.

519
00:16:28,721 --> 00:16:31,298
Slow and steady feels like losing.

520
00:16:31,478 --> 00:16:34,462
Social comparison pulls people off their plan,

521
00:16:34,462 --> 00:16:36,993
toward riskier bets or bigger spending.

522
00:16:37,342 --> 00:16:39,981
The fix: remember you only see wins,

523
00:16:39,981 --> 00:16:42,766
not losses, and you only see spending,

524
00:16:42,766 --> 00:16:43,426
not debt.

525
00:16:43,606 --> 00:16:46,202
Compare yourself with your own progress last

526
00:16:46,202 --> 00:16:46,497
year.

527
00:16:46,677 --> 00:16:48,267
Did your savings rate go up?

528
00:16:48,447 --> 00:16:49,748
Did your net worth go up?

529
00:16:49,928 --> 00:16:52,087
That's the only scoreboard that matters.

530
00:16:52,437 --> 00:16:53,633
Reason five.

531
00:16:53,813 --> 00:16:55,311
All or nothing thinking.

532
00:16:55,491 --> 00:16:58,420
People decide to save thirty percent starting

533
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:00,242
tomorrow, cut every expense,

534
00:17:00,242 --> 00:17:01,609
and invest perfectly.

535
00:17:01,789 --> 00:17:03,242
Then a hard month comes,

536
00:17:03,242 --> 00:17:04,392
they miss the goal,

537
00:17:04,392 --> 00:17:05,481
and quit entirely.

538
00:17:05,661 --> 00:17:07,194
Perfection is fragile.

539
00:17:07,543 --> 00:17:09,830
The fix: start smaller than you think.

540
00:17:10,010 --> 00:17:11,707
Researchers at Stanford,

541
00:17:11,707 --> 00:17:12,979
including BJ Fogg,

542
00:17:12,979 --> 00:17:15,170
have written about tiny habits:

543
00:17:15,170 --> 00:17:18,492
behaviors so small they're almost impossible to

544
00:17:18,492 --> 00:17:18,846
skip.

545
00:17:19,026 --> 00:17:20,407
Save one percent.

546
00:17:20,587 --> 00:17:22,584
Invest twenty five dollars.

547
00:17:22,764 --> 00:17:23,774
Then grow it.

548
00:17:23,954 --> 00:17:25,992
A small habit kept for a decade

549
00:17:25,992 --> 00:17:27,832
beats a big habit kept for a

550
00:17:27,832 --> 00:17:28,227
month.

551
00:17:28,576 --> 00:17:29,644
Reason six.

552
00:17:29,824 --> 00:17:31,540
It takes longer than you think to

553
00:17:31,540 --> 00:17:32,216
form a habit.

554
00:17:32,396 --> 00:17:35,058
A study by Phillippa Lally and colleagues

555
00:17:35,058 --> 00:17:38,110
at University College London found that forming

556
00:17:38,110 --> 00:17:40,448
a new habit took anywhere from about

557
00:17:40,448 --> 00:17:43,046
eighteen to over two hundred fifty days,

558
00:17:43,046 --> 00:17:45,318
with an average of around sixty six

559
00:17:45,318 --> 00:17:45,643
days.

560
00:17:45,823 --> 00:17:48,379
Most people expect it to feel automatic

561
00:17:48,379 --> 00:17:49,689
after a week or two.

562
00:17:49,869 --> 00:17:50,694
When it doesn't,

563
00:17:50,694 --> 00:17:52,087
they assume they've failed.

564
00:17:52,436 --> 00:17:55,053
The fix: give yourself at least three

565
00:17:55,053 --> 00:17:55,548
months.

566
00:17:55,728 --> 00:17:58,503
Expect the first weeks to feel effortful.

567
00:17:58,683 --> 00:17:59,437
That's normal.

568
00:17:59,617 --> 00:18:01,331
And missing a single day or a

569
00:18:01,331 --> 00:18:03,518
single month doesn't erase the habit.

570
00:18:03,698 --> 00:18:05,995
The same study found that missing one

571
00:18:05,995 --> 00:18:08,976
occasion didn't significantly hurt the long term

572
00:18:08,976 --> 00:18:09,410
result.

573
00:18:09,590 --> 00:18:10,566
Just restart.

574
00:18:10,915 --> 00:18:11,902
Reason seven.

575
00:18:12,082 --> 00:18:13,475
Lifestyle creep.

576
00:18:13,655 --> 00:18:14,793
As income grows,

577
00:18:14,793 --> 00:18:16,429
spending grows with it,

578
00:18:16,429 --> 00:18:19,274
and habit three stops feeding habits one

579
00:18:19,274 --> 00:18:19,843
and two.

580
00:18:20,023 --> 00:18:22,728
We covered this in my lifestyle inflation

581
00:18:22,728 --> 00:18:23,123
video.

582
00:18:23,303 --> 00:18:25,854
The fix: decide before the raise arrives

583
00:18:25,854 --> 00:18:27,576
what share goes to savings.

584
00:18:27,756 --> 00:18:29,126
Half is a great rule.

585
00:18:29,475 --> 00:18:30,578
And reason eight.

586
00:18:30,758 --> 00:18:31,548
Identity.

587
00:18:31,728 --> 00:18:34,843
Many people don't see themselves as investors

588
00:18:34,843 --> 00:18:35,536
or savers.

589
00:18:35,716 --> 00:18:37,860
They think wealth is for other kinds

590
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:38,456
of people.

591
00:18:38,636 --> 00:18:39,923
Writer James Clear,

592
00:18:39,923 --> 00:18:41,683
in his book Atomic Habits,

593
00:18:41,683 --> 00:18:44,528
argues that lasting habits often come from

594
00:18:44,528 --> 00:18:45,137
identity.

595
00:18:45,317 --> 00:18:47,170
Every small action is a vote for

596
00:18:47,170 --> 00:18:48,906
the type of person you want to

597
00:18:48,906 --> 00:18:49,311
become.

598
00:18:49,661 --> 00:18:52,411
The fix: start saying I'm someone who

599
00:18:52,411 --> 00:18:53,748
saves and invests.

600
00:18:53,928 --> 00:18:55,893
Then prove it with a tiny action,

601
00:18:55,893 --> 00:18:56,250
today.

602
00:18:56,430 --> 00:18:58,798
Each automatic transfer is a vote.

603
00:18:58,978 --> 00:19:00,608
After a few hundred votes,

604
00:19:00,608 --> 00:19:02,113
it's simply who you are.

605
00:19:02,463 --> 00:19:03,392
Quick pause.

606
00:19:03,572 --> 00:19:05,227
If this is helping you think about

607
00:19:05,227 --> 00:19:07,369
money differently, subscribe to the channel.

608
00:19:07,549 --> 00:19:09,409
It's free, and it helps me keep

609
00:19:09,409 --> 00:19:11,090
making these videos for you.

610
00:19:11,270 --> 00:19:13,517
Now, let's set up all three habits

611
00:19:13,517 --> 00:19:14,509
in thirty days.

612
00:19:14,858 --> 00:19:15,706
Angle three.

613
00:19:15,886 --> 00:19:16,664
The playbook.

614
00:19:16,844 --> 00:19:18,511
Here's how to set up each habit

615
00:19:18,511 --> 00:19:20,071
so it runs without willpower.

616
00:19:20,421 --> 00:19:21,861
Week one, habit one.

617
00:19:22,041 --> 00:19:23,632
Find your real numbers.

618
00:19:23,812 --> 00:19:25,548
Add up your take home pay and

619
00:19:25,548 --> 00:19:27,584
your spending from the last two or

620
00:19:27,584 --> 00:19:28,363
three months.

621
00:19:28,543 --> 00:19:30,458
Calculate your current savings rate.

622
00:19:30,638 --> 00:19:31,474
Don't judge it.

623
00:19:31,654 --> 00:19:32,386
Just know it.

624
00:19:32,566 --> 00:19:35,177
Then pick a starting target slightly above

625
00:19:35,177 --> 00:19:35,364
it.

626
00:19:35,544 --> 00:19:37,224
If you save nothing today,

627
00:19:37,224 --> 00:19:38,710
start with one percent,

628
00:19:38,710 --> 00:19:40,907
or twenty five dollars a paycheck.

629
00:19:41,257 --> 00:19:42,116
Then automate it.

630
00:19:42,296 --> 00:19:44,720
Set an automatic transfer from checking to

631
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:46,855
a separate savings account on the day

632
00:19:46,855 --> 00:19:48,125
your paycheck arrives.

633
00:19:48,305 --> 00:19:50,582
If you don't have an emergency fund

634
00:19:50,582 --> 00:19:53,185
yet, that's where this money goes first,

635
00:19:53,185 --> 00:19:55,332
until you have at least one month

636
00:19:55,332 --> 00:19:57,349
of expenses, then three to six.

637
00:19:57,698 --> 00:19:59,475
Week two, habit two.

638
00:19:59,655 --> 00:20:02,255
Open or review your investment accounts.

639
00:20:02,435 --> 00:20:04,752
If your employer offers a retirement plan

640
00:20:04,752 --> 00:20:05,487
with a match,

641
00:20:05,487 --> 00:20:08,030
make sure you're contributing at least enough

642
00:20:08,030 --> 00:20:09,274
to get the full match.

643
00:20:09,454 --> 00:20:11,172
If you're already doing that,

644
00:20:11,172 --> 00:20:13,603
turn on the automatic increase feature if

645
00:20:13,603 --> 00:20:14,669
your plan has one,

646
00:20:14,669 --> 00:20:17,040
so your contribution rises a little each

647
00:20:17,040 --> 00:20:17,337
year.

648
00:20:17,686 --> 00:20:19,314
Then choose simple,

649
00:20:19,314 --> 00:20:22,226
diversified, low cost investments.

650
00:20:22,406 --> 00:20:24,950
Many people use broad index funds or

651
00:20:24,950 --> 00:20:26,223
target date funds,

652
00:20:26,223 --> 00:20:28,838
which automatically adjust over time.

653
00:20:29,018 --> 00:20:31,897
Check the expense ratio of each fund.

654
00:20:32,077 --> 00:20:33,807
Lower is generally better.

655
00:20:33,987 --> 00:20:36,709
And set up automatic monthly contributions,

656
00:20:36,709 --> 00:20:39,495
so investing happens whether you think about

657
00:20:39,495 --> 00:20:40,129
it or not.

658
00:20:40,478 --> 00:20:42,197
Week three, habit three.

659
00:20:42,377 --> 00:20:44,349
Pick one income move for the next

660
00:20:44,349 --> 00:20:45,186
twelve months.

661
00:20:45,366 --> 00:20:47,693
Ask for a raise with clear evidence

662
00:20:47,693 --> 00:20:48,756
of your results.

663
00:20:48,936 --> 00:20:51,525
Research what your role pays elsewhere.

664
00:20:51,705 --> 00:20:54,098
Sign up for one certification or course

665
00:20:54,098 --> 00:20:55,815
that's valued in your field.

666
00:20:55,995 --> 00:20:58,513
Or start a small side income with

667
00:20:58,513 --> 00:20:59,734
a specific goal.

668
00:20:59,914 --> 00:21:01,504
One move, not ten.

669
00:21:01,854 --> 00:21:03,271
Then write your raise rule.

670
00:21:03,451 --> 00:21:05,997
For example, half of every raise goes

671
00:21:05,997 --> 00:21:07,718
to savings and investing,

672
00:21:07,718 --> 00:21:10,196
automatically, the month it arrives.

673
00:21:10,376 --> 00:21:12,150
Put it somewhere you'll see it,

674
00:21:12,150 --> 00:21:14,267
so future you doesn't forget when the

675
00:21:14,267 --> 00:21:15,984
excitement of a raise arrives.

676
00:21:16,333 --> 00:21:17,901
Week four, protection.

677
00:21:18,081 --> 00:21:19,927
Write your note for market drops.

678
00:21:20,107 --> 00:21:22,255
Check that your emergency fund is growing.

679
00:21:22,435 --> 00:21:24,900
List any high interest debt and choose

680
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:25,809
a payoff plan,

681
00:21:25,809 --> 00:21:28,924
because interest at twenty percent works against

682
00:21:28,924 --> 00:21:29,702
compounding.

683
00:21:29,882 --> 00:21:32,183
And schedule one money review every three

684
00:21:32,183 --> 00:21:32,576
months.

685
00:21:32,756 --> 00:21:34,474
Same day, same time.

686
00:21:34,654 --> 00:21:36,059
Fifteen minutes.

687
00:21:36,409 --> 00:21:37,988
Week one, save the gap.

688
00:21:38,168 --> 00:21:40,467
Week two, invest automatically.

689
00:21:40,647 --> 00:21:42,783
Week three, one income move.

690
00:21:42,963 --> 00:21:44,844
Week four, protect the plan.

691
00:21:45,194 --> 00:21:47,338
Even good habits can be broken by

692
00:21:47,338 --> 00:21:48,573
a few big mistakes.

693
00:21:48,753 --> 00:21:49,693
Mistake one.

694
00:21:49,873 --> 00:21:53,205
Cashing out retirement accounts when changing jobs.

695
00:21:53,385 --> 00:21:55,812
It can trigger taxes and penalties and

696
00:21:55,812 --> 00:21:57,600
resets years of compounding.

697
00:21:57,780 --> 00:21:59,637
Roll the money over instead.

698
00:21:59,987 --> 00:22:01,079
Mistake two.

699
00:22:01,259 --> 00:22:03,247
Taking on a big new fixed cost,

700
00:22:03,247 --> 00:22:05,170
like a large car payment or an

701
00:22:05,170 --> 00:22:07,671
expensive house, that forces you to cut

702
00:22:07,671 --> 00:22:09,467
your savings rate for years.

703
00:22:09,647 --> 00:22:11,017
And mistake three.

704
00:22:11,197 --> 00:22:12,542
Chasing a big win,

705
00:22:12,542 --> 00:22:15,307
like putting a large share of savings

706
00:22:15,307 --> 00:22:16,801
into a single stock,

707
00:22:16,801 --> 00:22:19,341
a coin, or a scheme promising fast

708
00:22:19,341 --> 00:22:19,939
returns.

709
00:22:20,119 --> 00:22:22,621
One big loss can undo a decade

710
00:22:22,621 --> 00:22:23,788
of slow gains.

711
00:22:24,138 --> 00:22:26,867
Here's a practical trick from habit research.

712
00:22:27,047 --> 00:22:28,556
It's called habit stacking.

713
00:22:28,736 --> 00:22:30,625
You attach a new habit to something

714
00:22:30,625 --> 00:22:32,406
you already do every day or every

715
00:22:32,406 --> 00:22:32,730
month.

716
00:22:33,079 --> 00:22:33,950
For example.

717
00:22:34,130 --> 00:22:35,664
When my paycheck arrives,

718
00:22:35,664 --> 00:22:38,180
I check that the automatic transfers went

719
00:22:38,180 --> 00:22:38,426
out.

720
00:22:38,606 --> 00:22:39,622
When I pay rent,

721
00:22:39,622 --> 00:22:41,465
I look at my savings rate for

722
00:22:41,465 --> 00:22:42,100
the month.

723
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:43,527
When I get a raise,

724
00:22:43,527 --> 00:22:47,005
I immediately raise my retirement contribution before

725
00:22:47,005 --> 00:22:48,120
I spend anything.

726
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:50,415
Each new habit rides on an existing

727
00:22:50,415 --> 00:22:52,470
one, so you don't have to remember

728
00:22:52,470 --> 00:22:53,316
it separately.

729
00:22:53,665 --> 00:22:56,231
You can stack small daily habits too.

730
00:22:56,411 --> 00:22:58,158
When I make morning coffee,

731
00:22:58,158 --> 00:23:00,811
I spend two minutes checking my accounts.

732
00:23:00,991 --> 00:23:03,107
When I want to buy something online

733
00:23:03,107 --> 00:23:04,256
over fifty dollars,

734
00:23:04,256 --> 00:23:05,646
I add it to a wish list

735
00:23:05,646 --> 00:23:06,553
and wait a day.

736
00:23:06,733 --> 00:23:09,554
These tiny routines protect the three big

737
00:23:09,554 --> 00:23:11,411
habits from slowly eroding.

738
00:23:11,761 --> 00:23:14,252
Habit stacking works because it uses cues

739
00:23:14,252 --> 00:23:16,196
that already exist in your life.

740
00:23:16,376 --> 00:23:18,013
You don't need more willpower.

741
00:23:18,193 --> 00:23:19,552
You need better triggers.

742
00:23:19,732 --> 00:23:22,065
And payday is the best money trigger

743
00:23:22,065 --> 00:23:24,203
there is, because it happens on a

744
00:23:24,203 --> 00:23:26,860
predictable schedule, every single month,

745
00:23:26,860 --> 00:23:27,638
for decades.

746
00:23:27,988 --> 00:23:29,833
Let's see what these habits look like

747
00:23:29,833 --> 00:23:30,879
in an ordinary month.

748
00:23:31,059 --> 00:23:33,869
On payday, three things happen automatically.

749
00:23:34,049 --> 00:23:36,115
Ten percent goes to the retirement plan

750
00:23:36,115 --> 00:23:36,963
through payroll.

751
00:23:37,143 --> 00:23:39,266
Five percent goes to a high yield

752
00:23:39,266 --> 00:23:40,939
savings account for goals.

753
00:23:41,119 --> 00:23:42,931
And a fixed amount goes to an

754
00:23:42,931 --> 00:23:45,055
index fund in a brokerage account.

755
00:23:45,235 --> 00:23:47,395
All before any spending happens.

756
00:23:47,744 --> 00:23:48,748
During the month,

757
00:23:48,748 --> 00:23:50,755
spending happens from what's left,

758
00:23:50,755 --> 00:23:53,412
without guilt, because the future has already

759
00:23:53,412 --> 00:23:54,002
been paid.

760
00:23:54,182 --> 00:23:56,643
There's no daily tracking of every coffee.

761
00:23:56,823 --> 00:23:58,510
Just a quick weekly look at the

762
00:23:58,510 --> 00:24:00,958
checking balance to make sure nothing strange

763
00:24:00,958 --> 00:24:01,665
is happening.

764
00:24:02,014 --> 00:24:03,019
Once a quarter,

765
00:24:03,019 --> 00:24:04,627
a fifteen minute review.

766
00:24:04,807 --> 00:24:06,676
Is the savings rate on track?

767
00:24:06,856 --> 00:24:08,121
Are fees low?

768
00:24:08,301 --> 00:24:09,753
Any new high interest debt?

769
00:24:09,933 --> 00:24:11,001
Any raise coming?

770
00:24:11,181 --> 00:24:12,418
And once a year,

771
00:24:12,418 --> 00:24:14,352
a slightly bigger review,

772
00:24:14,352 --> 00:24:17,833
including taxes, insurance and beneficiaries.

773
00:24:18,013 --> 00:24:18,408
That's it.

774
00:24:18,588 --> 00:24:21,296
Building wealth on autopilot takes less time

775
00:24:21,296 --> 00:24:23,574
than most people spend scrolling in a

776
00:24:23,574 --> 00:24:24,497
single evening.

777
00:24:24,847 --> 00:24:26,652
If you want to start right now,

778
00:24:26,652 --> 00:24:28,631
here are ten small actions you can

779
00:24:28,631 --> 00:24:29,155
do today.

780
00:24:29,335 --> 00:24:31,297
Pick one, or pick all ten.

781
00:24:31,646 --> 00:24:31,913
One.

782
00:24:32,093 --> 00:24:33,823
Calculate your savings rate.

783
00:24:34,003 --> 00:24:34,352
Two.

784
00:24:34,532 --> 00:24:37,004
Set up a twenty five dollar automatic

785
00:24:37,004 --> 00:24:38,340
transfer to savings.

786
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:39,077
Three.

787
00:24:39,257 --> 00:24:41,248
Check if you're getting your full employer

788
00:24:41,248 --> 00:24:41,533
match.

789
00:24:41,713 --> 00:24:42,235
Four.

790
00:24:42,415 --> 00:24:44,996
Turn on automatic increase in your retirement

791
00:24:44,996 --> 00:24:45,283
plan.

792
00:24:45,463 --> 00:24:45,788
Five.

793
00:24:45,968 --> 00:24:48,210
Look up the expense ratio of every

794
00:24:48,210 --> 00:24:49,068
fund you own.

795
00:24:49,417 --> 00:24:49,940
Six.

796
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:51,989
Write your note for market drops.

797
00:24:52,169 --> 00:24:52,610
Seven.

798
00:24:52,790 --> 00:24:54,067
Write your raise rule.

799
00:24:54,247 --> 00:24:54,538
Eight.

800
00:24:54,718 --> 00:24:56,654
List any high interest debt with its

801
00:24:56,654 --> 00:24:56,923
rate.

802
00:24:57,103 --> 00:24:57,661
Nine.

803
00:24:57,841 --> 00:24:59,420
Pick one income move for the year.

804
00:24:59,600 --> 00:25:00,261
And ten.

805
00:25:00,441 --> 00:25:02,290
Put a quarterly money review on your

806
00:25:02,290 --> 00:25:02,752
calendar.

807
00:25:02,932 --> 00:25:04,832
None of these takes more than thirty

808
00:25:04,832 --> 00:25:05,254
minutes.

809
00:25:05,434 --> 00:25:07,724
Together, they set up the three habits

810
00:25:07,724 --> 00:25:08,267
for life.

811
00:25:08,616 --> 00:25:11,217
Here's a comparison that surprises people.

812
00:25:11,397 --> 00:25:12,639
Two professionals.

813
00:25:12,819 --> 00:25:15,355
One earns two hundred thousand dollars a

814
00:25:15,355 --> 00:25:17,256
year but saves almost nothing,

815
00:25:17,256 --> 00:25:18,524
because a big house,

816
00:25:18,524 --> 00:25:21,249
nice cars and private everything absorb the

817
00:25:21,249 --> 00:25:22,200
whole paycheck.

818
00:25:22,380 --> 00:25:25,214
The other earns seventy thousand and saves

819
00:25:25,214 --> 00:25:27,104
fifteen percent, every year,

820
00:25:27,104 --> 00:25:29,870
automatically, and invests it in low cost

821
00:25:29,870 --> 00:25:30,275
funds.

822
00:25:30,624 --> 00:25:31,791
After twenty years,

823
00:25:31,791 --> 00:25:34,001
the high earner may have a beautiful

824
00:25:34,001 --> 00:25:36,764
lifestyle, and very little that would survive

825
00:25:36,764 --> 00:25:37,439
a job loss.

826
00:25:37,619 --> 00:25:38,701
The steady saver,

827
00:25:38,701 --> 00:25:41,181
at fifteen percent of seventy thousand,

828
00:25:41,181 --> 00:25:43,916
around eight hundred seventy five dollars a

829
00:25:43,916 --> 00:25:45,379
month at seven percent,

830
00:25:45,379 --> 00:25:48,113
could have over four hundred fifty thousand

831
00:25:48,113 --> 00:25:49,195
dollars invested.

832
00:25:49,375 --> 00:25:50,988
The habit beat the income.

833
00:25:51,338 --> 00:25:53,474
Income gives you the potential to build

834
00:25:53,474 --> 00:25:53,858
wealth.

835
00:25:54,038 --> 00:25:56,371
Habits decide whether you actually do.

836
00:25:56,551 --> 00:25:58,695
That's why the three habits work at

837
00:25:58,695 --> 00:26:00,287
almost every income level,

838
00:26:00,287 --> 00:26:02,370
and why a high income without them

839
00:26:02,370 --> 00:26:03,227
often doesn't.

840
00:26:03,577 --> 00:26:04,564
Objection one.

841
00:26:04,744 --> 00:26:05,950
This is too slow,

842
00:26:05,950 --> 00:26:08,007
I want to enjoy my money now.

843
00:26:08,187 --> 00:26:08,698
Fair.

844
00:26:08,878 --> 00:26:09,551
And you should.

845
00:26:09,731 --> 00:26:11,481
The habits don't ask you to give

846
00:26:11,481 --> 00:26:12,192
up enjoyment.

847
00:26:12,372 --> 00:26:13,993
They ask you to save a share

848
00:26:13,993 --> 00:26:16,134
first, and enjoy the rest guilt free.

849
00:26:16,314 --> 00:26:18,420
Many people find they enjoy spending more

850
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:20,424
when they know their future is handled.

851
00:26:20,773 --> 00:26:21,900
Objection two.

852
00:26:22,080 --> 00:26:23,798
The market is too risky.

853
00:26:23,978 --> 00:26:26,282
Markets are risky in the short term,

854
00:26:26,282 --> 00:26:28,651
which is why the emergency fund comes

855
00:26:28,651 --> 00:26:30,956
first, and why money you need within

856
00:26:30,956 --> 00:26:33,196
a few years shouldn't be in stocks.

857
00:26:33,376 --> 00:26:34,922
But for long term money,

858
00:26:34,922 --> 00:26:37,562
history suggests the bigger risk is often

859
00:26:37,562 --> 00:26:38,915
not investing at all,

860
00:26:38,915 --> 00:26:40,911
and losing ground to inflation.

861
00:26:41,261 --> 00:26:42,527
And objection three.

862
00:26:42,707 --> 00:26:44,692
I'll start when things settle down.

863
00:26:44,872 --> 00:26:46,486
Things rarely settle down.

864
00:26:46,666 --> 00:26:48,555
There's always a new expense,

865
00:26:48,555 --> 00:26:50,509
a new worry or a new headline.

866
00:26:50,689 --> 00:26:52,776
The habits are designed to work in

867
00:26:52,776 --> 00:26:53,882
an unsettled life.

868
00:26:54,062 --> 00:26:55,883
Start small, start now,

869
00:26:55,883 --> 00:26:58,021
and adjust as life changes.

870
00:26:58,370 --> 00:27:00,626
Let's see the three habits with three

871
00:27:00,626 --> 00:27:01,052
people.

872
00:27:01,232 --> 00:27:02,463
Rosa, twenty four,

873
00:27:02,463 --> 00:27:05,128
earns forty two thousand dollars a year

874
00:27:05,128 --> 00:27:06,701
as a medical assistant.

875
00:27:06,881 --> 00:27:09,419
She starts by saving just fifty dollars

876
00:27:09,419 --> 00:27:12,088
a month and contributing three percent to

877
00:27:12,088 --> 00:27:14,756
her retirement plan to get her employer's

878
00:27:14,756 --> 00:27:15,147
match.

879
00:27:15,327 --> 00:27:16,674
It feels tiny.

880
00:27:16,854 --> 00:27:18,455
For the first five years,

881
00:27:18,455 --> 00:27:20,569
her balance barely seems to move.

882
00:27:20,918 --> 00:27:22,915
But Rosa follows habit three.

883
00:27:23,095 --> 00:27:24,717
She earns a certification,

884
00:27:24,717 --> 00:27:27,025
then another, and moves into a better

885
00:27:27,025 --> 00:27:27,774
paying role.

886
00:27:27,954 --> 00:27:30,227
Each time, half of the raise goes

887
00:27:30,227 --> 00:27:30,984
to savings.

888
00:27:31,164 --> 00:27:32,227
By thirty five,

889
00:27:32,227 --> 00:27:34,921
she's saving fifteen percent of a much

890
00:27:34,921 --> 00:27:35,913
higher income.

891
00:27:36,093 --> 00:27:37,377
By her late forties,

892
00:27:37,377 --> 00:27:39,753
the growth on her investments in some

893
00:27:39,753 --> 00:27:42,258
years is larger than her contributions.

894
00:27:42,438 --> 00:27:44,330
Slowly, then suddenly.

895
00:27:44,680 --> 00:27:46,183
Marcus, thirty eight,

896
00:27:46,183 --> 00:27:49,115
starts later, with some credit card debt.

897
00:27:49,295 --> 00:27:50,868
He clears the debt first,

898
00:27:50,868 --> 00:27:53,635
then redirects the same monthly payment into

899
00:27:53,635 --> 00:27:54,264
investing.

900
00:27:54,444 --> 00:27:56,735
Because he's used to paying that amount,

901
00:27:56,735 --> 00:27:57,823
he doesn't miss it.

902
00:27:58,003 --> 00:27:59,917
He has fewer years than Rosa,

903
00:27:59,917 --> 00:28:01,963
so his savings rate needs to be

904
00:28:01,963 --> 00:28:04,734
higher, and he uses catch up contributions

905
00:28:04,734 --> 00:28:05,526
after fifty.

906
00:28:05,706 --> 00:28:07,065
His curve is shorter,

907
00:28:07,065 --> 00:28:08,748
but it still bends upward.

908
00:28:09,098 --> 00:28:10,709
And Helen, fifty two,

909
00:28:10,709 --> 00:28:12,396
has saved very little.

910
00:28:12,576 --> 00:28:14,173
She can't change the past,

911
00:28:14,173 --> 00:28:16,262
but she has her peak earning years

912
00:28:16,262 --> 00:28:18,473
ahead and the kids are nearly grown.

913
00:28:18,653 --> 00:28:20,671
She saves a large share of her

914
00:28:20,671 --> 00:28:22,420
income for thirteen years,

915
00:28:22,420 --> 00:28:23,497
keeps costs low,

916
00:28:23,497 --> 00:28:25,447
and plans to work a few years

917
00:28:25,447 --> 00:28:27,465
longer than she once expected.

918
00:28:27,645 --> 00:28:29,180
Her result is more modest,

919
00:28:29,180 --> 00:28:31,717
but it's the difference between a stressful

920
00:28:31,717 --> 00:28:33,369
retirement and a stable one.

921
00:28:33,719 --> 00:28:34,625
Three people.

922
00:28:34,805 --> 00:28:36,128
Three starting points.

923
00:28:36,308 --> 00:28:37,713
The same three habits.

924
00:28:37,893 --> 00:28:39,391
None of them got rich quickly.

925
00:28:39,571 --> 00:28:42,218
All of them got significantly richer slowly.

926
00:28:42,398 --> 00:28:43,596
And for each of them,

927
00:28:43,596 --> 00:28:45,593
the hardest part was the beginning,

928
00:28:45,593 --> 00:28:47,761
when the habits felt pointless and the

929
00:28:47,761 --> 00:28:49,074
results were invisible.

930
00:28:49,423 --> 00:28:51,449
People who reach the steep part of

931
00:28:51,449 --> 00:28:53,951
the curve often describe the same feeling.

932
00:28:54,131 --> 00:28:56,175
One day they check their account and

933
00:28:56,175 --> 00:28:57,993
realize it grew more in a single

934
00:28:57,993 --> 00:28:59,810
good year than they used to earn

935
00:28:59,810 --> 00:29:00,377
in a year.

936
00:29:00,557 --> 00:29:02,253
Or they notice that a bad month

937
00:29:02,253 --> 00:29:04,222
in the market moved their balance by

938
00:29:04,222 --> 00:29:06,246
more than their annual contributions.

939
00:29:06,426 --> 00:29:08,754
It's a strange mix of excitement and

940
00:29:08,754 --> 00:29:09,724
responsibility.

941
00:29:10,073 --> 00:29:12,407
But the most common feeling isn't excitement.

942
00:29:12,587 --> 00:29:13,237
It's calm.

943
00:29:13,417 --> 00:29:15,962
Money stops being a source of daily

944
00:29:15,962 --> 00:29:16,471
stress.

945
00:29:16,651 --> 00:29:18,531
Job decisions get easier.

946
00:29:18,711 --> 00:29:21,045
Emergencies become inconveniences.

947
00:29:21,225 --> 00:29:23,488
The suddenly part of wealth isn't really

948
00:29:23,488 --> 00:29:24,336
about a number.

949
00:29:24,516 --> 00:29:26,410
It's about freedom arriving,

950
00:29:26,410 --> 00:29:28,777
after years of quietly building it.

951
00:29:29,127 --> 00:29:31,860
And here's the part almost everyone says,

952
00:29:31,860 --> 00:29:32,726
looking back.

953
00:29:32,906 --> 00:29:35,026
I wish I had started earlier,

954
00:29:35,026 --> 00:29:36,122
even with less.

955
00:29:36,302 --> 00:29:38,687
Not because the early amounts were large,

956
00:29:38,687 --> 00:29:40,780
but because the early years were the

957
00:29:40,780 --> 00:29:42,874
ones that made the curve bend later.

958
00:29:43,054 --> 00:29:45,088
If you're at the start right now,

959
00:29:45,088 --> 00:29:46,630
this is your early years.

960
00:29:46,810 --> 00:29:47,367
Use them.

961
00:29:47,716 --> 00:29:49,970
Let me answer the questions people ask

962
00:29:49,970 --> 00:29:51,571
me most about these habits.

963
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:52,803
Question one.

964
00:29:52,983 --> 00:29:54,910
What if I can't save anything right

965
00:29:54,910 --> 00:29:55,131
now?

966
00:29:55,311 --> 00:29:57,224
Then start with habit three,

967
00:29:57,224 --> 00:30:00,368
growing income, and with cutting one recurring

968
00:30:00,368 --> 00:30:00,709
cost.

969
00:30:00,889 --> 00:30:02,788
Even ten dollars a week matters,

970
00:30:02,788 --> 00:30:04,331
not because of the amount,

971
00:30:04,331 --> 00:30:06,230
but because it builds the habit.

972
00:30:06,410 --> 00:30:07,596
When income grows,

973
00:30:07,596 --> 00:30:08,848
the habit is ready.

974
00:30:09,197 --> 00:30:10,149
Question two.

975
00:30:10,329 --> 00:30:12,382
Should I pay off debt or invest

976
00:30:12,382 --> 00:30:12,779
first?

977
00:30:12,959 --> 00:30:14,323
A common order is:

978
00:30:14,323 --> 00:30:16,066
a small emergency fund,

979
00:30:16,066 --> 00:30:18,263
then grab any employer match,

980
00:30:18,263 --> 00:30:20,688
then pay off high interest debt,

981
00:30:20,688 --> 00:30:23,188
then build a full emergency fund,

982
00:30:23,188 --> 00:30:24,476
then invest more.

983
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:25,873
Low interest debt,

984
00:30:25,873 --> 00:30:27,224
like some mortgages,

985
00:30:27,224 --> 00:30:29,590
can often be paid on schedule while

986
00:30:29,590 --> 00:30:30,333
you invest.

987
00:30:30,683 --> 00:30:31,751
Question three.

988
00:30:31,931 --> 00:30:33,726
How do I stay motivated during the

989
00:30:33,726 --> 00:30:34,253
slow part?

990
00:30:34,433 --> 00:30:35,879
Track your savings rate,

991
00:30:35,879 --> 00:30:38,830
celebrate milestones like your first ten thousand

992
00:30:38,830 --> 00:30:41,119
dollars, and remember the shape of the

993
00:30:41,119 --> 00:30:41,481
curve.

994
00:30:41,661 --> 00:30:43,428
The slow part is not a sign

995
00:30:43,428 --> 00:30:44,540
it isn't working.

996
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:47,204
It's a sign it's working exactly as

997
00:30:47,204 --> 00:30:47,843
expected.

998
00:30:48,193 --> 00:30:49,087
Question four.

999
00:30:49,267 --> 00:30:51,705
Is seven percent a realistic return?

1000
00:30:51,885 --> 00:30:54,562
It's an illustration often used for a

1001
00:30:54,562 --> 00:30:57,889
diversified stock portfolio over long periods,

1002
00:30:57,889 --> 00:30:59,118
before inflation.

1003
00:30:59,298 --> 00:31:00,995
Real returns vary a lot,

1004
00:31:00,995 --> 00:31:03,540
and some decades are much worse than

1005
00:31:03,540 --> 00:31:04,105
average.

1006
00:31:04,285 --> 00:31:06,499
That's why the habits matter more than

1007
00:31:06,499 --> 00:31:07,489
the exact return.

1008
00:31:07,669 --> 00:31:09,159
You control the saving,

1009
00:31:09,159 --> 00:31:11,036
the cost and the consistency.

1010
00:31:11,386 --> 00:31:12,327
Question five.

1011
00:31:12,507 --> 00:31:14,684
What if the market crashes right after

1012
00:31:14,684 --> 00:31:15,142
I start?

1013
00:31:15,322 --> 00:31:17,323
Then you're buying at lower prices,

1014
00:31:17,323 --> 00:31:19,438
which can be an advantage for someone

1015
00:31:19,438 --> 00:31:20,524
with decades ahead.

1016
00:31:20,704 --> 00:31:22,677
It feels terrible in the moment,

1017
00:31:22,677 --> 00:31:25,329
but early crashes hurt young investors much

1018
00:31:25,329 --> 00:31:26,562
less than late ones,

1019
00:31:26,562 --> 00:31:29,522
because their biggest contributions are still in

1020
00:31:29,522 --> 00:31:30,200
the future.

1021
00:31:30,380 --> 00:31:31,611
Keep the habit going.

1022
00:31:31,961 --> 00:31:33,088
Question six.

1023
00:31:33,268 --> 00:31:35,153
Do I need a financial advisor for

1024
00:31:35,153 --> 00:31:35,439
this?

1025
00:31:35,619 --> 00:31:37,873
Many people can set up these habits

1026
00:31:37,873 --> 00:31:39,482
on their own with simple,

1027
00:31:39,482 --> 00:31:40,448
low cost funds.

1028
00:31:40,628 --> 00:31:41,705
If you want help,

1029
00:31:41,705 --> 00:31:43,478
consider a fee only advisor,

1030
00:31:43,478 --> 00:31:45,695
who charges a clear fee rather than

1031
00:31:45,695 --> 00:31:48,228
commissions, and ask whether they act as

1032
00:31:48,228 --> 00:31:48,988
a fiduciary.

1033
00:31:49,337 --> 00:31:50,533
And question seven.

1034
00:31:50,713 --> 00:31:51,874
Is it ever too late?

1035
00:31:52,054 --> 00:31:52,298
No.

1036
00:31:52,478 --> 00:31:53,965
The curve is shorter,

1037
00:31:53,965 --> 00:31:55,311
but it still bends.

1038
00:31:55,491 --> 00:31:57,785
Starting at fifty with high savings and

1039
00:31:57,785 --> 00:31:59,785
a few extra working years can make

1040
00:31:59,785 --> 00:32:00,785
a big difference.

1041
00:32:00,965 --> 00:32:02,881
The worst number of years to wait

1042
00:32:02,881 --> 00:32:03,577
is one more.

1043
00:32:03,927 --> 00:32:04,694
Quick recap.

1044
00:32:04,874 --> 00:32:07,167
Habit one: spend less than you earn

1045
00:32:07,167 --> 00:32:08,542
and automate the gap.

1046
00:32:08,722 --> 00:32:10,752
Your savings rate is the most important

1047
00:32:10,752 --> 00:32:11,741
number you control.

1048
00:32:11,921 --> 00:32:14,109
Habit two: invest consistently,

1049
00:32:14,109 --> 00:32:17,285
cheaply, and don't interrupt the compounding.

1050
00:32:17,465 --> 00:32:19,783
Habit three: keep growing your income,

1051
00:32:19,783 --> 00:32:21,675
and send half of every raise to

1052
00:32:21,675 --> 00:32:22,956
the first two habits.

1053
00:32:23,306 --> 00:32:25,427
And remember why it's hard:

1054
00:32:25,427 --> 00:32:27,156
boredom, present bias,

1055
00:32:27,156 --> 00:32:30,455
fear, comparison, all or nothing thinking,

1056
00:32:30,455 --> 00:32:32,104
slow habit formation,

1057
00:32:32,104 --> 00:32:34,382
lifestyle creep and identity.

1058
00:32:34,562 --> 00:32:36,705
Start tiny, automate everything,

1059
00:32:36,705 --> 00:32:38,714
give it at least three months,

1060
00:32:38,714 --> 00:32:39,852
and let the long,

1061
00:32:39,852 --> 00:32:42,329
flat start turn into the steep climb.

1062
00:32:42,509 --> 00:32:44,367
Slowly, then suddenly.

1063
00:32:44,717 --> 00:32:46,302
If you want the full step by

1064
00:32:46,302 --> 00:32:48,339
step plan, from your first dollar to

1065
00:32:48,339 --> 00:32:49,414
your first million,

1066
00:32:49,414 --> 00:32:50,546
it's in my playbook.

1067
00:32:50,726 --> 00:32:52,432
The link is in the description.

1068
00:32:52,612 --> 00:32:55,634
And watch my video on lifestyle inflation

1069
00:32:55,634 --> 00:32:56,002
next.

1070
00:32:56,182 --> 00:32:58,621
It's the biggest threat to habit three.

1071
00:32:58,801 --> 00:32:59,996
I'm Ryan Rover.

1072
00:33:00,176 --> 00:33:01,395
See you in the next one.
